{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1787865425973831992,"compiled_state":{"alias":"unhelping-creviced-rhino","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":2,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"cbe6b6f95c942651b3b6b483c373d00eed66932fa51f05620fd8ef4f3cd6600a","event_uuid":"3facf0b4-9741-4630-bed9-92bb12f72945","position":{"commit_sha":"aca1aaaa8b4e2e2e0fab7952713de82fb55749ec","position":"1787860170488898642-3facf0b4-9741-4630-bed9-92bb12f72945"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"273a1882016fee3e123f4a8ad05510dc11bcf442b3746de435052d096d70a921","event_uuid":"42fa7763-ef62-4fcf-a305-f280c79e304a","position":{"commit_sha":"a843b33ef24205bf97e0e1ae8cf2a5d2df10df85","position":"1787860177592963594-42fa7763-ef62-4fcf-a305-f280c79e304a"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1787860170488898642,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"42fa7763-ef62-4fcf-a305-f280c79e304a","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"6fe7-956f-4901-45cf"}],"description":"## Summary\n\nDiscovered by the Step 7 parity census while fixing bug `unreal-milky-sloth` (6fe7-956f-4901-45cf),\nwhich fixed the **MCP** surface only. The **CLI** `--output json` surface has the same exposure and was\ndeliberately left out of that fix's scope.\n\n## Evidence (measured, structural — parsed as JSON, not grepped from prose)\n\n```\n$ rebar show <ticket> --output json | python -c '<scan for |n| > 2**53-1>'\nCLI show --output json, STRUCTURAL unsafe ints: 3\n    $.created_at            = 1787856371950409998\n    $.comments[0].timestamp = 1787856976224609473\n    $.updated_at            = 1787857168766976021\n```\n\nRFC 8259 §6 guarantees implementations \"agree exactly on their numeric values\" only for integers in\n`[-(2**53)+1, (2**53)-1]`. Any float64-based consumer of this output — `jq`, Node, Ruby, most JSON\ntooling — silently rounds these. rebar already knows this and acted on it for its own event path:\n`src/rebar/_store/canonical.py:15-19` and `src/rebar/_store/hlc.py:24-25` (\"jq must never touch it (it\nparses as float64 and rounds)\"). The CLI's public JSON output is the one remaining surface where a user\n*is* invited to pipe rebar JSON into jq.\n\n## Why this was NOT fixed alongside the MCP bug\n\nDifferent consumer population and a stronger stated contract:\n\n- `docs/api-stability.md:23` calls `--output json` the **\"Strongest contract\"**.\n- CLI JSON consumers today (shell/jq/python pipelines) receive integers. Python consumers read them\n  losslessly and would break if handed strings without warning.\n- The MCP fix (`src/rebar/_mcp_errors.py` `js_safe_result` / `install_js_safe_guard`) is scoped to the\n  MCP tool-return seam precisely so CLI/library JSON is unchanged.\n\nSo this is a real but distinct decision, not a leftover of the same change.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide and record the policy for CLI `--output json`: keep bare integers (and document the jq\n      hazard prominently for users), or emit the JS/float64-safe decimal-string form, or add a flag.\n- [ ] If the representation changes, `docs/api-stability.md` and the affected `src/rebar/schemas/*.json`\n      are updated together, and the change is called out as a consumer-visible break.\n- [ ] Regression coverage pinning whichever behaviour is chosen, so the CLI surface cannot drift silently.\n\n## Notes\n\nAlso probed and found clean / not applicable:\n- Library facade (`import rebar`) — returns Python ints; Python integers are arbitrary-precision and\n  there is no JSON boundary. N/A.\n- Reconciler/Jira outbound payloads — searched for nanosecond integers in outbound JSON and found none.\n  Not an exhaustive audit.\n","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":3,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"e127-a3ad-895a-4a2f","ticket_type":"bug","title":"CLI `--output json` emits nanosecond timestamps as bare >2^53 JSON numbers (float64 consumers round them)","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["3facf0b4-9741-4630-bed9-92bb12f72945","42fa7763-ef62-4fcf-a305-f280c79e304a"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787865425973831992,"uuid":"389dd617-9536-4d7b-a7a0-6cab48ad8284"}